  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 127 AM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nNot much change from the previous forecast. Expect drier conditions \nto continue through the week outside of isolated seabreeze showers \nand thunderstorms. That is due to weak mid level ridging and a \nslightly drier airmass across S TX. However, a broad mid level \ntrough draped across the gulf waters and a broad surface low across \nthe Bay of Campeche will continue to bring a medium to high chance \nof showers and thunderstorms along the Middle Texas Coast and \nCoastal Waters through the end of the work week. The National \nHurricane Center expects a tropical storm to develop overnight and \neventually becoming a hurricane as we progress through the week. The \nstorm is thankfully expected to move north-northeast, keeping the \nsystem far east of S TX. This will result in drier conditions on the \nwest side of the system. Though again we cannot rule out an isolated \nmorning or sea breeze shower each day. The drier conditions may also \nunfortunately lead to much warmer temperatures across S TX. A \nmid/upper level cut off low is progged to track/develop across West \nTX which would provide upper support for convective development \nacross Mexico on Thu/Fri. Though with the lack of moisture in the \nRio Grande Plains chances will remain very low at this time for \nconvection.\n\nThe main concern if a tropical system develops over the gulf will be \nan increased risk of coastal flooding, moderate to high rip current \nrisk and seas possibly building up to 9-10 feet. Will not extend the \nadvisory through tonight and not expecting coastal flooding on \nduring today either as winds in the gulf waters will be lighter.\nAdditional Coastal Flood Advisories may be needed the latter part of \nthe week depending on tropical development in the gulf. Some models \nindicate swell periods may increase to 9-10 seconds by Friday along \nwith seas building to 9-10 feet or higher due to a strengthening \nnortheast flow over the gulf waters. This will provide sufficient \nenergy to push the water up to the dunes. If the winds strengthen to \n25-30 knots with seas building to around 12 feet, then tide levels \nat high tide could reach 3 feet MSL and a Coastal Flood Warning \nwould be needed. However, chances are low that this will occur, but \nwill continue to monitor trends and possible tropical development.",
